{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HzHanx5bZ8BVVQXieVrBWZ","type":"tool_result","content":"478\t\n479\t**Checkpoint: You've Now Confirmed**\n480\t✓ You can separate frozen from deprecated, and state the practical consequence\n481\t✓ You resolve a homonym by asking whose scope the stem is in, before reading options\n482\t\n483\tTwo sections of content behind you. The rest of this chapter is strategy: how to spend ninety minutes, where to spend your remaining study hours, and what to do with the last week.\n484\t\n485\t---\n486\t\n487\t## ⚪ §3 — Ninety Minutes\n488\t\n489\tThe Linux Foundation allows **90 minutes** for its multiple-choice exams [source: lf-mc-exam-faq-2026-08-31]. That number is published, on the KCNA exam page and in the candidate handbook, and it does not move. Two further facts shape everything in this section:\n490\t\n491\t**The timer cannot be paused.** \"The system does not offer a way to pause the exam timer or to add time back to the exam during connection loss events\" [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31]. Not for a bathroom break, not for a dropped connection. Ninety minutes is a hard, unrecoverable budget.\n492\t\n493\t**There is no scratch paper.** \"Candidate is not allowed to write or enter input on anything (whether paper, electronic device, etc.) outside of the Exam console screen\" [source: lf-exam-rules-and-policies-2026-08-31]. You cannot write your pacing plan down when the clock starts. Whatever plan you use has to be executable from memory, which is the entire reason the rule below is short enough to memorize.\n494\t\n495\t### The rule\n496\t\n497\t> **★ Fixed Point**\n498\t>\n499\t> **Read the question count off the screen. Divide the clock by it. Bank the first pass at roughly 60% of your total time, and reserve the rest for flagged questions.**\n500\t>\n501\t> On a 90-minute exam, that means the first pass through every question ends at roughly the 54-minute mark, leaving about 36 minutes for everything you flagged. You do not need to know the question count in advance — you read it, you divide, you go.\n502\t\n503\tThis is stated as a rule rather than a seconds-per-question number on purpose. The Linux Foundation publishes a 60-question format for its multiple-choice exams, in the candidate handbook, for that class of exam [source: lf-mc-exam-important-instructions-2026-08-31]. As a worked example: 60 questions in 90 minutes is 90 seconds apiece, and a first pass banked at 54 minutes gives you about 54 seconds per question on the way through, with the balance held back. But the rule is what you memorize, because the rule survives a different count and the arithmetic does not.\n504\t\n505\t> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** The 60-question and 75% figures are published — in the Linux Foundation's *candidate handbook*, for multiple-choice exams as a class, not on the KCNA product page you probably read first [source: provenance-kcna-60-questions-2026-08-31]. That gap between where a fact lives and where a candidate looks is why the numbers circulate for years looking unsourced. Read the handbook, not just the product page.\n506\t\n507\t### The first pass\n508\t\n509\tThree categories, decided fast:\n510\t\n511\t**Know it.** Answer, move. Do not re-read to confirm. Confirmation on a question you knew is the single largest source of time leakage on a timed exam, and it almost never changes the answer.\n512\t\n513\t**Can get it.** Two options survive, and you can see the discriminator if you think for twenty seconds. Give it the twenty seconds. If it resolves, answer and move. If it does not, pick the more likely one, flag it, move.\n514\t\n515\t**Don't know it.** Answer anyway. There is no penalty for a wrong answer that leaving it blank would avoid. Flag it and move immediately. Do not sit with it. The cost of sitting is that you spend three minutes on one question and then rush four you would have got.\n516\t\n517\tAlways leave an answer even when you flag. A flagged question with an answer already in it is a question you can improve; a flagged blank is a question you can lose to the clock.\n518\t\n519\t### The second pass\n520\t\n521\tYour flagged questions, in the order you flagged them. Two things have changed since you first saw them:\n522\t\n523\tYou have read the whole exam, and later questions sometimes disambiguate earlier ones — a stem that names a concept precisely can tell you which sense an earlier ambiguous stem meant. And you are no longer under first-pass momentum, so you can afford the full discriminator procedure from §2.\n524\t\n525\t**Change an answer only when you can say why.** \"It feels wrong now\" is not a reason; it is fatigue. \"I misread the scope: it said RoleBinding, not ClusterRoleBinding\" is a reason. This distinction matters more than the folk wisdom about never changing answers, which is wrong: changing an answer for an articulable reason improves your score, and changing it on a feeling does not.\n526\t\n527\t### The three ways candidates run out of time\n528\t\n529\tThey have different fixes, which is why they are worth separating.\n530\t\n531\t**Re-reading stems.** You read the question, read it again, then read it a third time before touching an option. Fix: read once, carefully, then go to the options. If the options reveal you missed something, you can come back, but you will usually find that reading the options *is* what clarifies the stem.\n532\t\n533\t**Refusing to flag.** You will not leave a question unresolved, so you spend four minutes fighting one item. Fix: the flag is a tool, and using it is not surrender. A flagged question you return to with a clear head is worth more than the same question fought into the ground at minute nineteen.\n534\t\n535\t**Re-litigating settled answers.** You keep returning to questions you already answered confidently. Fix: the second pass is for *flagged* questions. If you did not flag it, you do not revisit it.\n536\t\n537\t<!-- FIGURE: ch19-fig03-exam-day-pacing -->"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"42d6f0ae-7b0a-48db-a252-693e8a9bb7f6","uuid":"e8905b89-0f06-4d40-8c73-16f72e7a7272","timestamp":"2026-08-31T22:57:51.212Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-19\\draft-v1.md","content":"\n**Checkpoint: You've Now Confirmed**\n✓ You can separate frozen from deprecated, and state the practical consequence\n✓ You resolve a homonym by asking whose scope the stem is in, before reading options\n\nTwo sections of content behind you. The rest of this chapter is strategy: how to spend ninety minutes, where to spend your remaining study hours, and what to do with the last week.\n\n---\n\n## ⚪ §3 — Ninety Minutes\n\nThe Linux Foundation allows **90 minutes** for its multiple-choice exams [source: lf-mc-exam-faq-2026-08-31]. That number is published, on the KCNA exam page and in the candidate handbook, and it does not move. Two further facts shape everything in this section:\n\n**The timer cannot be paused.** \"The system does not offer a way to pause the exam timer or to add time back to the exam during connection loss events\" [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31]. Not for a bathroom break, not for a dropped connection. Ninety minutes is a hard, unrecoverable budget.\n\n**There is no scratch paper.** \"Candidate is not allowed to write or enter input on anything (whether paper, electronic device, etc.) outside of the Exam console screen\" [source: lf-exam-rules-and-policies-2026-08-31]. You cannot write your pacing plan down when the clock starts. Whatever plan you use has to be executable from memory, which is the entire reason the rule below is short enough to memorize.\n\n### The rule\n\n> **★ Fixed Point**\n>\n> **Read the question count off the screen. Divide the clock by it. Bank the first pass at roughly 60% of your total time, and reserve the rest for flagged questions.**\n>\n> On a 90-minute exam, that means the first pass through every question ends at roughly the 54-minute mark, leaving about 36 minutes for everything you flagged. You do not need to know the question count in advance — you read it, you divide, you go.\n\nThis is stated as a rule rather than a seconds-per-question number on purpose. The Linux Foundation publishes a 60-question format for its multiple-choice exams, in the candidate handbook, for that class of exam [source: lf-mc-exam-important-instructions-2026-08-31]. As a worked example: 60 questions in 90 minutes is 90 seconds apiece, and a first pass banked at 54 minutes gives you about 54 seconds per question on the way through, with the balance held back. But the rule is what you memorize, because the rule survives a different count and the arithmetic does not.\n\n> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** The 60-question and 75% figures are published — in the Linux Foundation's *candidate handbook*, for multiple-choice exams as a class, not on the KCNA product page you probably read first [source: provenance-kcna-60-questions-2026-08-31]. That gap between where a fact lives and where a candidate looks is why the numbers circulate for years looking unsourced. Read the handbook, not just the product page.\n\n### The first pass\n\nThree categories, decided fast:\n\n**Know it.** Answer, move. Do not re-read to confirm. Confirmation on a question you knew is the single largest source of time leakage on a timed exam, and it almost never changes the answer.\n\n**Can get it.** Two options survive, and you can see the discriminator if you think for twenty seconds. Give it the twenty seconds. If it resolves, answer and move. If it does not, pick the more likely one, flag it, move.\n\n**Don't know it.** Answer anyway. There is no penalty for a wrong answer that leaving it blank would avoid. Flag it and move immediately. Do not sit with it. The cost of sitting is that you spend three minutes on one question and then rush four you would have got.\n\nAlways leave an answer even when you flag. A flagged question with an answer already in it is a question you can improve; a flagged blank is a question you can lose to the clock.\n\n### The second pass\n\nYour flagged questions, in the order you flagged them. Two things have changed since you first saw them:\n\nYou have read the whole exam, and later questions sometimes disambiguate earlier ones — a stem that names a concept precisely can tell you which sense an earlier ambiguous stem meant. And you are no longer under first-pass momentum, so you can afford the full discriminator procedure from §2.\n\n**Change an answer only when you can say why.** \"It feels wrong now\" is not a reason; it is fatigue. \"I misread the scope: it said RoleBinding, not ClusterRoleBinding\" is a reason. This distinction matters more than the folk wisdom about never changing answers, which is wrong: changing an answer for an articulable reason improves your score, and changing it on a feeling does not.\n\n### The three ways candidates run out of time\n\nThey have different fixes, which is why they are worth separating.\n\n**Re-reading stems.** You read the question, read it again, then read it a third time before touching an option. Fix: read once, carefully, then go to the options. If the options reveal you missed something, you can come back, but you will usually find that reading the options *is* what clarifies the stem.\n\n**Refusing to flag.** You will not leave a question unresolved, so you spend four minutes fighting one item. Fix: the flag is a tool, and using it is not surrender. A flagged question you return to with a clear head is worth more than the same question fought into the ground at minute nineteen.\n\n**Re-litigating settled answers.** You keep returning to questions you already answered confidently. Fix: the second pass is for *flagged* questions. If you did not flag it, you do not revisit it.\n\n<!-- FIGURE: ch19-fig03-exam-day-pacing -->","numLines":60,"startLine":478,"totalLines":940}}}
