{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015zVtpY1sy4yevhZJmUagiv","type":"tool_result","content":"555\t**One more thing about the environment**, because it costs points every year: the exam is closed-book. \"Candidates are NOT PERMITTED to access tools, resources or external sites when taking the Linux Foundation Multiple Choice OR SkillCred Exams\" [source: lf-certification-resources-allowed-2026-08-31]. If you have read CKA preparation advice about having kubernetes.io open in a browser tab, that allowance is real — for the *performance-based* exams, which permit browsing the Kubernetes documentation during the exam [source: lf-certification-resources-allowed-2026-08-31]. It does not transfer to KCNA. Nothing is open. Plan accordingly.\n556\t\n557\t---\n558\t\n559\t## ⚪ §4 — Where the Weight Actually Is\n560\t\n561\tStudy time is not fungible with comfort. The hours you most want to spend are usually on material you already know, because reviewing what you know feels good and reviewing what you do not feels like failing. The stretch of water you most enjoy sailing is rarely the one that puts you on the rocks. This section is a correction for that.\n562\t\n563\t> **Dead Reckoning:** The four domains and their published weights are: Kubernetes Fundamentals 44%, Container Orchestration 28%, Cloud Native Application Delivery 16%, Cloud Native Architecture 12% [source: cncf-curriculum-kcna-readme-2026-08-31]. Kubernetes Fundamentals is Chapters 2–8. Container Orchestration is Chapters 9–13. Cloud Native Application Delivery is Chapters 14–16. Cloud Native Architecture is Chapters 17–18. These weights come from CNCF's own curriculum repository and agree exactly with the Linux Foundation exam page and the published curriculum PDF.\n564\t\n565\t### Build the table yourself\n566\t\n567\tDo not skip this. The value is in the filling-in, not the reading. You are going to see something about your own preparation that a printed table cannot show you.\n568\t\n569\tGo back through the book and record your **Soundings score** and your **Taking Your Bearings scores** for each chapter. Then complete this:\n570\t\n571\t| Domain | Weight | Chapters | Your worst Bearings score in this range | Which chapter | Hours you've spent here |\n572\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n573\t| D1 Kubernetes Fundamentals | 44% | 2–8 | | | |\n574\t| D2 Container Orchestration | 28% | 9–13 | | | |\n575\t| D3 Cloud Native App Delivery | 16% | 14–16 | | | |\n576\t| D4 Cloud Native Architecture | 12% | 17–18 | | | |\n577\t\n578\tThen answer one question: **is the domain where you scored worst the domain where you have spent the most hours?**\n579\t\n580\tIf the answer is no, and for most readers it is no, that gap is your study plan. It is almost always more valuable than any general advice this book could give you, because it is measured on you.\n581\t\n582\t### Two calls this section has to make\n583\t\n584\t**Community and Collaboration is the reliably under-studied competency.** It sits inside Cloud Native Architecture (Chapter 17 §2 and §8) and it is where readers who ran out of time stopped. The reasons are consistent: it is institutional rather than technical, it is name-dense, and it does not *feel* examinable in the way a `kubectl` behavior does. It is examinable. It is on the published curriculum as one of Cloud Native Architecture's three competencies [source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23].\n585\t\n586\tIt also has the best ratio of exam presence to study time in the entire book. The material is finite and bounded: governance bodies and their remits, SIGs versus Working Groups versus Committees, the project lifecycle, the maturity levels. There is no depth to plumb. An hour spent there returns more than an hour spent adding nuance to a Domain 1 topic you already score well on.\n587\t\n588\t**The 2025-11-24 blueprint change is a study-allocation hazard, not just a historical fact.** The KCNA exam was updated no earlier than November 24, 2025 [source: lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23], and the change was not cosmetic: Cloud Native Application Delivery is now 16%, and observability was \"rolled under Cloud Native Architecture\" [source: lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23] rather than being its own domain.\n589\t\n590\tThe practical consequence: **third-party study material purchased before that date is mis-allocated by roughly a full domain's worth of weight.** If your practice questions came from a bank that still lists five domains, your sense of where the points are is wrong. And the rule for which blueprint you are tested against is unambiguous — \"Any KCNA exam taken after the updated release will test on the new set of Domains and Competencies,\" and \"The only date that matters is the date you sit for the exam\" [source: lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23]. Not your purchase date, not your first attempt, not your retake status.\n591\t\n592\t> 🪝 **Snag:** Check the copyright date on every non-CNCF resource you're using. A five-domain list is the tell. If you see \"Cloud Native Observability\" as a standalone domain with its own weight, that material predates the change.\n593\t\n594\t### Where the next block of hours goes\n595\t\n596\tIn priority order, and this order holds for most readers:\n597\t\n598\t1. **Your worst-scoring chapter in Domain 1.** It is 44% of the exam. A weak chapter there costs more than a weak chapter anywhere else, arithmetically.\n599\t2. **Community and Collaboration**, if you have not deliberately studied it. Highest return per hour in the book.\n600\t3. **§2 of this chapter**, worked actively rather than read. Discrimination failures are cheap to fix and expensive to leave.\n601\t4. **Your worst-scoring chapter in Domain 2.** 28% and the second-largest surface.\n602\t5. **A timed full-length attempt**, if you have not sat one. Pacing is a distinct skill and it is invisible until you meet the clock.\n603\t\n604\tWhat is *not* on that list: re-reading chapters you scored well on. It feels like studying. It is not.\n605\t\n606\t---"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"42d6f0ae-7b0a-48db-a252-693e8a9bb7f6","uuid":"c2047e43-ce38-49b7-ac07-e63a5e262630","timestamp":"2026-08-31T22:57:57.244Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-19\\draft-v1.md","content":"**One more thing about the environment**, because it costs points every year: the exam is closed-book. \"Candidates are NOT PERMITTED to access tools, resources or external sites when taking the Linux Foundation Multiple Choice OR SkillCred Exams\" [source: lf-certification-resources-allowed-2026-08-31]. If you have read CKA preparation advice about having kubernetes.io open in a browser tab, that allowance is real — for the *performance-based* exams, which permit browsing the Kubernetes documentation during the exam [source: lf-certification-resources-allowed-2026-08-31]. It does not transfer to KCNA. Nothing is open. Plan accordingly.\n\n---\n\n## ⚪ §4 — Where the Weight Actually Is\n\nStudy time is not fungible with comfort. The hours you most want to spend are usually on material you already know, because reviewing what you know feels good and reviewing what you do not feels like failing. The stretch of water you most enjoy sailing is rarely the one that puts you on the rocks. This section is a correction for that.\n\n> **Dead Reckoning:** The four domains and their published weights are: Kubernetes Fundamentals 44%, Container Orchestration 28%, Cloud Native Application Delivery 16%, Cloud Native Architecture 12% [source: cncf-curriculum-kcna-readme-2026-08-31]. Kubernetes Fundamentals is Chapters 2–8. Container Orchestration is Chapters 9–13. Cloud Native Application Delivery is Chapters 14–16. Cloud Native Architecture is Chapters 17–18. These weights come from CNCF's own curriculum repository and agree exactly with the Linux Foundation exam page and the published curriculum PDF.\n\n### Build the table yourself\n\nDo not skip this. The value is in the filling-in, not the reading. You are going to see something about your own preparation that a printed table cannot show you.\n\nGo back through the book and record your **Soundings score** and your **Taking Your Bearings scores** for each chapter. Then complete this:\n\n| Domain | Weight | Chapters | Your worst Bearings score in this range | Which chapter | Hours you've spent here |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 Kubernetes Fundamentals | 44% | 2–8 | | | |\n| D2 Container Orchestration | 28% | 9–13 | | | |\n| D3 Cloud Native App Delivery | 16% | 14–16 | | | |\n| D4 Cloud Native Architecture | 12% | 17–18 | | | |\n\nThen answer one question: **is the domain where you scored worst the domain where you have spent the most hours?**\n\nIf the answer is no, and for most readers it is no, that gap is your study plan. It is almost always more valuable than any general advice this book could give you, because it is measured on you.\n\n### Two calls this section has to make\n\n**Community and Collaboration is the reliably under-studied competency.** It sits inside Cloud Native Architecture (Chapter 17 §2 and §8) and it is where readers who ran out of time stopped. The reasons are consistent: it is institutional rather than technical, it is name-dense, and it does not *feel* examinable in the way a `kubectl` behavior does. It is examinable. It is on the published curriculum as one of Cloud Native Architecture's three competencies [source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23].\n\nIt also has the best ratio of exam presence to study time in the entire book. The material is finite and bounded: governance bodies and their remits, SIGs versus Working Groups versus Committees, the project lifecycle, the maturity levels. There is no depth to plumb. An hour spent there returns more than an hour spent adding nuance to a Domain 1 topic you already score well on.\n\n**The 2025-11-24 blueprint change is a study-allocation hazard, not just a historical fact.** The KCNA exam was updated no earlier than November 24, 2025 [source: lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23], and the change was not cosmetic: Cloud Native Application Delivery is now 16%, and observability was \"rolled under Cloud Native Architecture\" [source: lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23] rather than being its own domain.\n\nThe practical consequence: **third-party study material purchased before that date is mis-allocated by roughly a full domain's worth of weight.** If your practice questions came from a bank that still lists five domains, your sense of where the points are is wrong. And the rule for which blueprint you are tested against is unambiguous — \"Any KCNA exam taken after the updated release will test on the new set of Domains and Competencies,\" and \"The only date that matters is the date you sit for the exam\" [source: lf-kcna-program-changes-2026-08-23]. Not your purchase date, not your first attempt, not your retake status.\n\n> 🪝 **Snag:** Check the copyright date on every non-CNCF resource you're using. A five-domain list is the tell. If you see \"Cloud Native Observability\" as a standalone domain with its own weight, that material predates the change.\n\n### Where the next block of hours goes\n\nIn priority order, and this order holds for most readers:\n\n1. **Your worst-scoring chapter in Domain 1.** It is 44% of the exam. A weak chapter there costs more than a weak chapter anywhere else, arithmetically.\n2. **Community and Collaboration**, if you have not deliberately studied it. Highest return per hour in the book.\n3. **§2 of this chapter**, worked actively rather than read. Discrimination failures are cheap to fix and expensive to leave.\n4. **Your worst-scoring chapter in Domain 2.** 28% and the second-largest surface.\n5. **A timed full-length attempt**, if you have not sat one. Pacing is a distinct skill and it is invisible until you meet the clock.\n\nWhat is *not* on that list: re-reading chapters you scored well on. It feels like studying. It is not.\n\n---","numLines":52,"startLine":555,"totalLines":940}}}
