{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qtu1UknXdfyUHAUhe6rY5n","type":"tool_result","content":"644\t---\n645\t\n646\t## ⚪ §6 — The Week Before\n647\t\n648\tThe last week is the one most likely to be spent badly, because anxiety pushes toward *more*: more material, more sources, more hours. More is the wrong direction. Consolidation, not acquisition.\n649\t\n650\t### Seven days out\n651\t\n652\t**Sit one full-length timed attempt.** Ninety minutes, no pauses, nothing open, in one sitting. Not to score — to calibrate. You want to know whether you finish with time to spare, finish exactly on the buzzer, or run out. Those three outcomes have completely different fixes and you cannot guess which one you are.\n653\t\n654\t**Review the wrong answers properly.** For each one, name *why*: knowledge gap, discrimination failure (a §2 pair), misread stem, or clock. The four have different remedies, and sorting them is worth more than the raw score.\n655\t\n656\t### Six to three days out\n657\t\n658\t**Work the two or three chapters your attempt flagged.** Not the whole book. Named chapters, from measured evidence.\n659\t\n660\t**Work §2 of this chapter actively.** Cover the right column, say the discriminator out loud, check. Twenty minutes a day. Discrimination degrades faster than knowledge and refreshes faster too.\n661\t\n662\t**Give Community and Collaboration a dedicated hour** if you have not. See §4.\n663\t\n664\t**Check your equipment now, not on the morning.** This is a genuinely important use of a final-week hour, because every item here can fail and every one takes time to fix. You need a supported OS, one active monitor (\"Dual Monitors are NOT supported\"), a webcam you can move to pan the room, a microphone, and reliable internet; a wired connection is more stable than wireless [source: lf-handbook2-candidate-requirements-2026-08-31]. You cannot use a virtual machine [source: lf-mc-exam-important-instructions-2026-08-31]. And avoid work-provided devices: the Linux Foundation states plainly that they \"can result in technical challenges\" [source: lf-mc-exam-faq-2026-08-31], which usually means a managed endpoint blocking the secure browser at exactly the wrong moment.\n665\t\n666\t**Check your ID against your registration.** The first and last name on your government-issued photo ID must exactly match the name on your Linux Foundation account [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31]. A mismatch discovered at check-in is not something a proctor can wave through.\n667\t\n668\t### Two days out\n669\t\n670\t**Prepare the room.** The space must be private, clutter-free, and well lit, with no papers or writing implements on the desk and none below it, and no printouts on the walls; décor is fine, notes are not [source: lf-handbook2-candidate-requirements-2026-08-31]. Public spaces are prohibited outright. If the room has a door, it must be closed. Sorting this two days out costs ten minutes; sorting it while a check-in specialist waits costs composure you would rather spend on the exam.\n671\t\n672\t**Stop adding sources.** No new question banks, no new videos, no new guides. A new source in the last 48 hours does not add knowledge; it adds a fresh sense of everything you do not know, which is the opposite of what you need.\n673\t\n674\t### The day before\n675\t\n676\t**Do not study new material.** This is where Community and Collaboration typically gets crammed, and typically does not stick. Institutional vocabulary learned in a state of anxiety twelve hours before an exam is exactly the kind of memory that does not survive retrieval under pressure. If you did not learn it in week one, you are not learning it in hour ninety.\n677\t\n678\t**One Lodestar pass in the morning, then stop.** Twenty minutes.\n679\t\n680\t**Rest.** This sounds like the sort of advice a study guide includes because study guides include it. It is not. A tired reader misreads stems, and misread stems are one of the four failure modes you sorted your wrong answers into a week ago. Sleep is a direct intervention on that failure mode, and it is the only one available to you on the last night.\n681\t\n682\t### The morning\n683\t\n684\tCheck in early — the portal opens up to 30 minutes before your scheduled time, and you must start no later than 30 minutes after it [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31]. Expect a wait for a check-in specialist; it \"should not exceed 15 minutes\" [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31], but building that in means it is not a surprise. You will upload a photo of your ID and take a selfie for comparison [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31], and you will pan the room with your webcam.\n685\t\n686\tThen, when the exam is released: read the count off the screen, divide, and go.\n687\t\n688\t> **Logbook Entry:** The final week has a texture that nobody warns you about, so here it is.\n689\t>\n690\t> Around day four, you will have a bad session. You will get a run of practice questions wrong on material you were confident about three days earlier, and the conclusion that will arrive uninvited is that you have been fooling yourself and you are not ready. This is close to universal and it is close to meaningless. What is actually happening is that you have moved from recognition practice to retrieval practice, and retrieval is harder. It is *supposed* to feel harder, and the difficulty is what makes the memory durable. Recognition was always the easier task and it was always flattering you.\n691\t>\n692\t> The failure mode is what people do next. Faced with a bad session, the instinct is to widen: buy another question bank, find a different video series, start a fresh set of notes. Every one of those is a way of avoiding the specific thing that went wrong, which is generally three or four discriminations that have gone soft and could be repaired in forty minutes with the material already in front of you.\n693\t>\n694\t> The other thing worth knowing: on exam day you will encounter a question you have no idea about. Not a hard question — a question about something you would swear was never covered. Everyone does. It is one question out of sixty, it is worth the same as every other question, and the only mistake available to you is letting it take four minutes and your composure. Answer it, flag it, move. You will very likely never think about it again, because you will have passed.\n695\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"42d6f0ae-7b0a-48db-a252-693e8a9bb7f6","uuid":"e6aed82f-8fd2-4737-862f-e54ce2f6b927","timestamp":"2026-08-31T22:57:57.732Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-19\\draft-v1.md","content":"---\n\n## ⚪ §6 — The Week Before\n\nThe last week is the one most likely to be spent badly, because anxiety pushes toward *more*: more material, more sources, more hours. More is the wrong direction. Consolidation, not acquisition.\n\n### Seven days out\n\n**Sit one full-length timed attempt.** Ninety minutes, no pauses, nothing open, in one sitting. Not to score — to calibrate. You want to know whether you finish with time to spare, finish exactly on the buzzer, or run out. Those three outcomes have completely different fixes and you cannot guess which one you are.\n\n**Review the wrong answers properly.** For each one, name *why*: knowledge gap, discrimination failure (a §2 pair), misread stem, or clock. The four have different remedies, and sorting them is worth more than the raw score.\n\n### Six to three days out\n\n**Work the two or three chapters your attempt flagged.** Not the whole book. Named chapters, from measured evidence.\n\n**Work §2 of this chapter actively.** Cover the right column, say the discriminator out loud, check. Twenty minutes a day. Discrimination degrades faster than knowledge and refreshes faster too.\n\n**Give Community and Collaboration a dedicated hour** if you have not. See §4.\n\n**Check your equipment now, not on the morning.** This is a genuinely important use of a final-week hour, because every item here can fail and every one takes time to fix. You need a supported OS, one active monitor (\"Dual Monitors are NOT supported\"), a webcam you can move to pan the room, a microphone, and reliable internet; a wired connection is more stable than wireless [source: lf-handbook2-candidate-requirements-2026-08-31]. You cannot use a virtual machine [source: lf-mc-exam-important-instructions-2026-08-31]. And avoid work-provided devices: the Linux Foundation states plainly that they \"can result in technical challenges\" [source: lf-mc-exam-faq-2026-08-31], which usually means a managed endpoint blocking the secure browser at exactly the wrong moment.\n\n**Check your ID against your registration.** The first and last name on your government-issued photo ID must exactly match the name on your Linux Foundation account [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31]. A mismatch discovered at check-in is not something a proctor can wave through.\n\n### Two days out\n\n**Prepare the room.** The space must be private, clutter-free, and well lit, with no papers or writing implements on the desk and none below it, and no printouts on the walls; décor is fine, notes are not [source: lf-handbook2-candidate-requirements-2026-08-31]. Public spaces are prohibited outright. If the room has a door, it must be closed. Sorting this two days out costs ten minutes; sorting it while a check-in specialist waits costs composure you would rather spend on the exam.\n\n**Stop adding sources.** No new question banks, no new videos, no new guides. A new source in the last 48 hours does not add knowledge; it adds a fresh sense of everything you do not know, which is the opposite of what you need.\n\n### The day before\n\n**Do not study new material.** This is where Community and Collaboration typically gets crammed, and typically does not stick. Institutional vocabulary learned in a state of anxiety twelve hours before an exam is exactly the kind of memory that does not survive retrieval under pressure. If you did not learn it in week one, you are not learning it in hour ninety.\n\n**One Lodestar pass in the morning, then stop.** Twenty minutes.\n\n**Rest.** This sounds like the sort of advice a study guide includes because study guides include it. It is not. A tired reader misreads stems, and misread stems are one of the four failure modes you sorted your wrong answers into a week ago. Sleep is a direct intervention on that failure mode, and it is the only one available to you on the last night.\n\n### The morning\n\nCheck in early — the portal opens up to 30 minutes before your scheduled time, and you must start no later than 30 minutes after it [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31]. Expect a wait for a check-in specialist; it \"should not exceed 15 minutes\" [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31], but building that in means it is not a surprise. You will upload a photo of your ID and take a selfie for comparison [source: lf-handbook2-taking-the-exam-2026-08-31], and you will pan the room with your webcam.\n\nThen, when the exam is released: read the count off the screen, divide, and go.\n\n> **Logbook Entry:** The final week has a texture that nobody warns you about, so here it is.\n>\n> Around day four, you will have a bad session. You will get a run of practice questions wrong on material you were confident about three days earlier, and the conclusion that will arrive uninvited is that you have been fooling yourself and you are not ready. This is close to universal and it is close to meaningless. What is actually happening is that you have moved from recognition practice to retrieval practice, and retrieval is harder. It is *supposed* to feel harder, and the difficulty is what makes the memory durable. Recognition was always the easier task and it was always flattering you.\n>\n> The failure mode is what people do next. Faced with a bad session, the instinct is to widen: buy another question bank, find a different video series, start a fresh set of notes. Every one of those is a way of avoiding the specific thing that went wrong, which is generally three or four discriminations that have gone soft and could be repaired in forty minutes with the material already in front of you.\n>\n> The other thing worth knowing: on exam day you will encounter a question you have no idea about. Not a hard question — a question about something you would swear was never covered. Everyone does. It is one question out of sixty, it is worth the same as every other question, and the only mistake available to you is letting it take four minutes and your composure. Answer it, flag it, move. You will very likely never think about it again, because you will have passed.\n","numLines":52,"startLine":644,"totalLines":940}}}
